## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design realistic cross-service end-to-end tests with real-time updates, failure modes, and proper data isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time event streams (WebSocket/SSE) and state machine validation
- Test isolation with per-tenant data and fresh environments
- Deterministic inputs via seed data and mocked external services
- Idempotency and eventual consistency verification across services
- Failure injection testing (disconnects, latency, partial outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch using Playwright
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test.describe('E2E Food Delivery Flow', () => {
  let wsServer, testData;

  test.beforeAll(async () => {
    wsServer = await startMockWebSocketServer();
    testData = await seedDeterministicTestData();
  });

  test('complete delivery flow with driver tracking', async ({ page }) => {
    const driverUpdates = [];

    // Simulate real-time driver location stream
    wsServer.on('driver-location', (data) => driverUpdates.push(data));

    // 1. Place order
    await page.goto('/order');
    await page.fill('#address', testData.address);
    await page.click('#place-order');
    const orderId = await expectOrderConfirmed(page);

    // 2. Restaurant accepts
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'accepted' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'accepted');

    // 3. Driver assigned
    await wsServer.emit('driver-assigned', { orderId, driver: testData.driver });
    await expectDriverAssigned(page, testData.driver);

    // 4. Verify location/ETA streaming
    await wsServer.emit('driver-location', { lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060, eta: 10 });
    await expectETA(page, '10 min');

    // 5. Payment processed
    await stubPaymentAPI({ orderId, status: 'success' });
    await expectPaymentStatus(page, 'paid');

    // 6. Delivery completes
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'delivered');

    // Verify idempotent event handling
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    expect(driverUpdates).toHaveLength(1); // No duplicate processing

    // Verify eventual consistency
    await expectExternalServiceSynced(orderId);
  });

  test('handles driver disconnect gracefully', async ({ page }) => {
    const orderId = await placeOrder(page);
    await wsServer.simulateDisconnect();

    await expectReconnectionLogic(page);
    await expectDriverReassigned(page);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for real-time collaboration systems must handle offline edits, eventual consistency, and deterministic merges. This question evaluates your ability to architect an end-to-end flow that exercises offline queues, CRDT-based merging, and version history while ensuring test isolation and reproducibility.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first collaboration with optimistic UI updates
- CRDT-based merge algorithms ensuring deterministic outcomes
- Test data isolation using per-test seeds and unique identifiers
- Server-sync mocks with controlled timing for deterministic test results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Setup two Playwright contexts for concurrent user simulation
const contextA = await browser.newContext();
const contextB = await browser.newContext();
const pageA = await contextA.newPage();
const pageB = await contextB.newPage();

// Seed a shared base document
const docId = await seedDocument({ content: 'Initial paragraph.' });

// Simulate offline for both users
await setNetworkOffline(pageA);
await setNetworkOffline(pageB);

// User A edits the paragraph
await pageA.goto(`/doc/${docId}`);
await pageA.locator('.editor').fill('User A edited this.');

// User B edits the same paragraph offline
await pageB.goto(`/doc/${docId}`);
await pageB.locator('.editor').fill('User B edited that.');

// Reconnect both users to trigger CRDT sync
await setNetworkOnline(pageA);
await setNetworkOnline(pageB);

// Wait for sync completion
await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText('Expected merged content');
await expect(pageB.locator('.editor')).toHaveText('Expected merged content');

// Verify idempotent replay
const versionBefore = await getDocumentVersion(docId);
await triggerSyncAgain(pageA);
const versionAfter = await getDocumentVersion(docId);
expect(versionAfter).toBe(versionBefore);

// Verify version history
const history = await getVersionHistory(docId);
expect(history).toContain('User A edited this.');
expect(history).toContain('User B edited that.');
```

